  "gotchas": [
    {
      "severity": "warn",
      "text": "This is the integrity workload run on the same stack that the splice gotcha on this cell describes: 36 synthetic JavaScript prompts, temperature 0, one at a time, 47,138 output tokens in total. One generation (lgi-0016, line 61) came back with a spliced token, `0apse` where the value 0 was expected, so accuracy here is 35/36 = 0.972. Treat that as a floor, not the rate: the integrity scorer only flags splices that break an identifier, a number or a known word, and an earlier pass of the same 36 prompts (before the dataset gained its numeric tables) flagged two (`modeipse`, `0hed`). A 24-prompt probe kit with token_ids returned measured roughly one bad token per 8,000 output tokens on this profile."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "Runtime refusal-direction ablation is ENABLED for this cell (ablate=true, lambda 3.5, layers 10-42). It changes what the model is willing to answer, so any eval score sensitive to refusals is a score for the ablated model and not for the stock checkpoint."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "The served weights are a REAP-pruned derivative keeping 216 of 256 routed experts per layer, quantized to EXL3 trellis 3.0 bpw. Active parameters per token are unchanged at 20.4B, so the decode bandwidth bound matches the unpruned model; only the footprint falls."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "SparkInfer publishes no version. This cell names the short git commit of the configuration repository, which is what actually determines the build, because compose.yml pins the container by digest."
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "SparkInfer streams its thought block as delta.reasoning rather than delta.reasoning_content. A harness that reads only the two common spellings records every streaming request on this stack as an empty answer; atlas-bench counts all three since 2026-08-25."
    },
    {
      "severity": "warn",
      "text": "Long generations from this cell occasionally contain one spliced token: an identifier or number with a stray fragment glued on (`carrierhed`, `groupipse`, `128Pin`) or a number replaced by a bare word (`[6, visible, 0]`). Measured at roughly one bad token per 8,000 output tokens on 24 fixed ~1,600-token JavaScript prompts at temperature 0 (20 of 104 generations hit; same image digest, 384k-context single-sequence profile), and about one per 1,500 in a coding-agent session. The stray token is present in the sampled token_ids returned with `return_token_ids: true`, so it is upstream of the detokenizer, and decoding the ids locally with the model's tokenizer reproduces the server text exactly. Not changed by disabling speculative decoding, the refusal ablation, async scheduling, prefix caching, the FlashInfer sampler, or switching the indexer to native; the fp8 KV-cache A/B and the FlashInfer attention backend cannot be booted on this image (page-stride check 37376 < 37440; missing sparse MLA decode API). The short evals here do not see it because their outputs are short; anything that consumes 1k+ token outputs (code generation, agents) should expect it."
    }
  ],
